+#### 4.1 The command table
+
+§3, §4 and §5's per-command rules MUST be data, not code: one file per target,
+`targets/<id>/commands.toml`, read only from the package's targets folder (never from a
+path the caller, the environment, the working directory or a config file names; a file
+that resolves outside that folder is refused). One generic engine interprets it.
+
+1. Each row names commands (or a name prefix such as `mkfs.`), gives their argv grammar
+   (options without a value, options with a value in the short, attached and long `=`
+   forms, whether options may follow operands, `--`) and a list of effects from this
+   fixed set, each with an optional condition on the argv:
+   - `runs-command-at`: an inner command starts at a given operand, or after a given word
+     up to a terminator (`find -exec … ;`); it is classed as a simple command of its own,
+     with the outer command's stdin. A row with it MUST carry a minimal example prefix
+     (`timeout 5`), and for every such row `<prefix> rm -rf x` MUST be at least
+     `destructive` and `<prefix> sudo -i` as a proposal MUST be rejected;
+   - `runs-text`: runs shell text (`bash -c`, `eval`, `su -c`, `env -S`);
+   - `runs-stdin-as-code`: runs its stdin as shell code (`bash` with no operand, `bash -s`);
+   - `runs-file-as-code`: runs a file as shell code (`bash f`, `.`, `source`);
+   - `sets-variable`: assigns variables (`export`, `declare`, `read`, `printf -v`,
+     `mapfile`, `getopts`, `let`, `env NAME=v`), or changes other shell state (`alias`,
+     `set`, `trap`, …; always `session-altering`). An assignment to `SUDO_PROMPT` or
+     `SUDO_ASKPASS`, or to a name the gate cannot read, is rejected (§5); one to a §3 name
+     is `session-altering`;
+   - `elevates`: `privileged`; a pipe, here-document, here-string or a stdin the gate
+     cannot place into it is rejected, and so are its password options (§5);
+   - `destroys`: at least `destructive`;
+   - `reads-terminal`: reads the terminal when given no input (the §2 terminal-read rule);
+   - `opens-shell`: opens an interactive shell; with `elevates` on the command or its inner
+     command, a model proposal is rejected (§5).
+2. When the gate cannot read the argv (an option the row does not list, or a word in
+   option position that is not a literal), every condition of the row MUST be taken to
+   hold. A word in `sudo`'s option position that is not a literal may be `-S`, so it is
+   rejected.
+3. **Decision A (opaque command).** A simple command with no row and no allowlist entry is
+   at least `mutating`, reason "not allowlisted". If any argument word, after quote
+   removal, names a command whose row has `elevates`, the command is `privileged` and a
+   model proposal is rejected; if one names a row with `destroys`, it is at least
+   `destructive`. A word names a command by its last path component, and a command word
+   that is a path outside the system directories (`/usr/local/bin/sudo`, `./rm`) counts
+   too: it is not proven to be that command, but it names it.
+4. **Decision B (code the gate cannot read).** `runs-stdin-as-code` fed by a pipe, a file or
+   a stdin the gate cannot place, `runs-text` with text that is not literal, and
+   `runs-file-as-code` are at least `destructive`, reason "runs code the gate cannot read".
+   Literal text (a literal `-c` string, a literal here-string or quoted here-document) is
+   classed again with this whole procedure, within the same work budget.
+5. **Decision C (gate failure).** An unhandled node kind, the work budget exceeded (counted
+   in work units: nodes visited plus texts and commands classed again, across the whole
+   classification), a missing or invalid `commands.toml`, or any internal error: the
+   command is `privileged`, rejected, reason "could not class".
+6. Rows are reviewed like allowlist entries. Adding a row or an effect raises classes;
+   removing a row or an effect, or narrowing a condition, can lower a class, so it is a
+   v2 change (Versioning).
 
